// A DATED, INDEPENDENTLY TRANSCRIBED REALIZATION FIXTURE — NOT LIVE DRIFT DETECTION.
//
// Read this claim precisely, because an earlier revision of this note overstated it and the
// overstatement is the exact failure this repository keeps paying for. What executes here is
//
//     emitter(current) == bytes transcribed from srv1 on 2026-08-19
//
// and NOT
//
//     emitter(current) == host(current)
//
// Nothing reads srv1 at execution time. If the deployed units drift tomorrow while these
// functions stay put, every witness below stays green — so this fixture CANNOT ask "did the host
// change?", and calling the running host an external authority for these bytes would be a claim
// the mechanism does not support. The deployed file is an observed realization, not the authority
// for systemd syntax or for desired deployment state.
//
// What it WAS good for: the expected bytes were obtained independently of the functions under
// test, so a row transcribed from the host and left alone is not a self-capture golden — it
// cannot certify a renderer as correct merely because nobody edited it, which is the change
// detector DESIGN section 5 rejects. A row that still holds its transcription is a sound BASELINE
// for refactoring these renderers: a red means the emitter moved off the last observed deployed
// baseline, which during a migration is exactly the question.
//
// THAT PROPERTY IS NOW TRUE OF ONE ROW OUT OF FIVE, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM IT FOR ALL OF
// THEM. Independence is not a property of the fixture's design; it is a property of each row's
// EDIT HISTORY, and it is destroyed the moment an author updates an expected literal so that a
// code change goes green. Since the 2026-08-19 transcription (#8496, 8b20599960) that has happened
// three times:
//
//   4b7371d1f6 (#10357, 2026-09-04)  belt-service, serve, tree-sync edited; slice row ADDED
//   75e1cf68b3 (#10446, 2026-09-04)  belt-service edited
//   6a4ca1c5bc (#10590, 2026-09-05)  belt-service and serve edited (user-manager Environment)
//
// So per row, as of this writing: the_belt_timer_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes still holds its
// 2026-08-19 transcription and is the only row carrying an external referent. belt-service, serve
// and tree-sync have each been edited to follow the emitter and are now EMITTER-REGRESSION PINS —
// they still refuse an unintended emission change, which is worth keeping, but they are no longer
// evidence that the emitter agrees with anything outside this tree. The slice row never had a
// transcription at all: it was authored with the emitter in #10357.
//
// The decay is legible in a name: the_tree_sync_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes_PLUS_THE_EXCLUSION
// _WIDENING says out loud that its expectation is host bytes plus an emitter change.
//
// THE OBLIGATION THIS CREATES, stated rather than left to the next reader: an edit to an expected
// literal is a rung change on that row, not a maintenance chore. Either re-transcribe from the
// host and say so with a date, or move the row to a directive-level assertion over the typed
// SystemdUnitFile (gunbc.live_deploy.emit live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file and
// live_deploy_serve_unit_file return one before rendering), which asserts the DECISION — principal,
// slice, working tree, environment derivation — and cannot be satisfied by editing the expectation.
// What is forbidden is what happened here: editing the bytes while a paragraph three screens up
// keeps vouching for their independence. That is DESIGN section 4b rung inflation, and the
// inflated claim was load-bearing — it was cited to justify keeping these rows.
//
// It is needed because nothing else pins these bytes. dag/test/claim/live_deploy/emit_test.dag
// carries 57 witnesses and not one asserts a unit doc's text; the emitted deploy script is not a
// committed artifact, so git shows no diff; and the generated-artifact drift gate was deleted by
// the 2026-08-15 floor cut (.github/workflows now holds only fleet-converge.yml and witnesses.yml).
//
// Live drift detection is a DIFFERENT mechanism this fixture does not provide: it would have to
// observe the host at run time and would belong with the fleet observation lane, not here.
//
// ── RETIRED WITH THEIR MEMBER, NOT PENDING AN OBSERVATION ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// the_fleet_converge_service_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes and
// the_fleet_converge_timer_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes were transcribed from
// gunbc-srv1-green-fleet-converge.{service,timer}, which existed only on the deleted slot. srv1's
// surviving deployment carries no fleet-converge units — checked on the host 2026-08-19.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS RECEIPT GAVE A FALSE REASON, and the correction matters more than the
// rows. It said the members were still in deployment_spec_srv1's step list and would appear at the
// next apply, so the oracle would re-arm then. They are not. gunbc.live_deploy.spec
// deployment_owned_steps lists eight members and FleetConvergeTimerUnit is not among them —
// deployment_apply_order_note in that module records it as RETIRED from deployment membership by
// operator directive 2026-08-13, and test.claim.live_deploy.emit carries an enrolled claim
// asserting exactly that absence. So the re-arm trigger I wrote could never fire: no apply will
// ever install those units. That is the stalled-trigger shape DESIGN section 4b names, a `cannot
// climb` wearing the label `can climb after one event`, and it would have sent a later session
// waiting on an apply that cannot deliver.
//
// WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE: gunbc.live_deploy.emit emit_fleet_converge_service_unit_doc and
// emit_fleet_converge_timer_unit_doc now have no member, no spec consumer, and no oracle. They are
// orphaned emitters, and under delete-first they are the NEXT root to cut rather than evidence to
// re-arm. This is recorded as an observation for that cut, not as an obligation on this one — no
// trigger is declared here, because there is no event that would discharge it.
//
fn live_spec() -> DeploymentSpec {
  deployment_spec_srv1()
}

// The revision the live slot is actually serving, read from its ExecStart on the same date.
// Bound rather than synthesised because it appears verbatim in the emitted unit.
fn live_revision() -> ReleaseRevisionBinding {
  RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: "b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd" as CommitSha }
}

// gunbc-roadmap-belt.service, as deployed on srv1.
//
// THIS ROW AGREES WITH THE LIVE BYTES AGAIN, and the round trip is worth recording because the
// intermediate state was committed. An earlier cut added EnvironmentFile=-/etc/... here and annotated
// the row as DELIBERATELY DIVERGING until the next apply -- honest at the time, and describing a
// change that was then withdrawn: binding an ambient credential into this multipurpose unit hands the
// token to every child the belt spawns, including the dispatched provider session. See
// live_deploy.spec deployment_credential_env_path for why the withdrawal happened and what replaces
// it.
//
// WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE DEFECT THE OLD ANNOTATION NAMED: publish_pass still refuses on every tick
// with "GITHUB_TOKEN is not set in the belt's environment" (belt-last-tick.json, observed
// 2026-08-27). That refusal is UNCHANGED and is now correct rather than pending -- the belt is not
// supposed to hold that credential. It closes when the publication-only consumer boundary lands, not
// when this unit is next applied.
test fn the_belt_service_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_belt_service_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc roadmap belt tick (one reconcile pass over the ready frontier)\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-roadmap.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=GUNBC_BELT_SPAWN_WORKDIR=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nEnvironment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_belt_tick_cli.dag --function belt_run_once_cli\n"
}

// gunbc-roadmap-belt.timer, as deployed on srv1 2026-08-19.
test fn the_belt_timer_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_belt_timer_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc roadmap belt tick cadence\n\n[Timer]\nUnit=gunbc-roadmap-belt.service\nOnBootSec=2min\nOnUnitInactiveSec=60s\nAccuracySec=5s\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=timers.target\n"
}

// gunbc-roadmap.service, as deployed on srv1. LIKE THE BELT ROW ABOVE, this briefly carried an
// EnvironmentFile line and no longer does, for the same reason: POST /publish/NODE_ID reaches the
// same credential resolver the belt tick does (roadmap_serve
// serve_publish_handler_response_for_instance -> belt_publish_node_for_instance), so crediting THIS
// long-running multipurpose server was the same over-grant in a second place. Both routes will share
// one publication boundary rather than one ambient token; live_deploy.spec
// deployment_credential_env_path carries the argument.
test fn the_serve_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_systemd_unit_doc(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc roadmap HTTP server (gunbc serve)\nAfter=network-online.target tailscaled.service\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=simple\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-roadmap.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nEnvironment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag --function roadmap_serve_handle --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080 --release-revision b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd --eval-budget-cpu-ms 30000 --eval-budget-wall-ms 30000\nRestart=on-failure\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n"
}

// gunbc-tree-sync.service, AS EMITTED, and this row now asserts the emission rather than a
// transcription. Its predecessor kept a 2026-08-19 transcription of the deployed bytes carrying a
// SECOND ExecStart -- the `.git` rsync leg -- and named its one intended divergence, the widened
// exclude run on the first leg. The `.git` leg was deleted from the emitter when Git-native
// repository convergence replaced file transport of repository metadata (gunbc.live_deploy.
// repository_convergence_placement), and the expectation was never moved, so this witness was red
// on main against an emitter with one ExecStart and nothing rostered it. Measured 2026-09-04 while
// wiring `gunbc converge --mode deploy`.
//
// WHAT IS ASSERTED NOW: one ExecStart, the exclude run derived from deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes
// (eight prefixes: the two bootstrap receipt channels joined it the same day), and the deployment
// slice the unit joins. The deployed bytes on srv1 predate all three and converge on the next deploy.
test fn the_tree_sync_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes_plus_the_exclusion_widening() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_tree_sync_unit_doc(spec: live_spec(), tree_path: live_spec().service.repo_root as String), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc source tree sync (service-user-principal rsync from CI runner checkout)\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-roadmap.slice\nEnvironmentFile=/etc/gunbc-tree-sync.env\nExecStart=/usr/bin/rsync -rlpt --delete --exclude /target --exclude /.git --exclude /.claude --exclude /.gunbc --exclude /target-codex --exclude /.cargo-home --exclude /.gunbc-build-receipts --exclude /.gunbc-converge-receipts \"$\{GUNBC_TREE_SRC}/\" /opt/gunbc/gunbc/\n"
}


// THE SLICE UNIT, BYTE FOR BYTE. The bound is the runner slot's allowance rendered by the
// resource-control authority; a change to either shows up here as the deployed bytes changing.
test fn the_slice_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_slice_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc deployment cgroup bound for gunbc-roadmap.service\n\n[Slice]\nMemoryMax=17179869184\nMemoryHigh=16106127360\n"
}

// ── EXECUTED RED (DESIGN §4b) — mutate the spec, emit, show the positive claim fail ────────────

fn has_directive(dirs: List<SystemdServiceDirective>, target: SystemdServiceDirective) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, fn(d) { d == target })
}

fn any(xs: List<a>, pred: fn(a) -> Bool) -> Bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false
    [first, ..rest] => if pred(first) { true } else { any(xs: rest, pred: pred) }
  }
}

fn belt_service_directives_of(spec: DeploymentSpec) -> List<SystemdServiceDirective> {
  match live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: spec) {
    ServiceUnitFile { service: s, .. } => s
  }
}

fn tree_sync_service_directives_of(spec: DeploymentSpec) -> List<SystemdServiceDirective> {
  match live_deploy_tree_sync_unit_file(spec: spec, tree_path: spec.service.repo_root as String) {
    ServiceUnitFile { service: s, .. } => s
  }
}

fn spec_with_repo_root(wrong_root: NonEmptyStr) -> DeploymentSpec {
  let base = live_spec()
  match base {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
          instance_root: ir, service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: wrong_root,
              service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
              listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// RED: a spec with a different repo_root makes belt_service_works_in_repo_tree go red.
// The positive claim asserts WorkingDirectory == spec.service.repo_root.
// When repo_root changes, the emitted WorkingDirectory changes with it.
test fn belt_service_working_directory_goes_red_under_different_root() -> Bool {
  let wrong_root = join([live_spec().service.repo_root as String, "-staging"], "") as NonEmptyStr
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_repo_root(wrong_root: wrong_root)
  !has_directive(
    dirs: belt_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: WorkingDirectory { path: live_spec().service.repo_root })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: belt_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: WorkingDirectory { path: wrong_root })
}

// RED: a spec with a different repo_root makes tree_sync_execstart go red.
// The positive claim asserts ExecStart contains repo_root.
// When repo_root changes, the emitted ExecStart path changes with it.
test fn tree_sync_execstart_goes_red_under_different_root() -> Bool {
  let wrong_root = join([live_spec().service.repo_root as String, "-staging"], "") as NonEmptyStr
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_repo_root(wrong_root: wrong_root)
  let original_exec = join([
    "/usr/bin/rsync -rlpt --delete --exclude /target --exclude /.git --exclude /.claude",
    " --exclude /.gunbc --exclude /target-codex --exclude /.cargo-home",
    " --exclude /.gunbc-build-receipts --exclude /.gunbc-converge-receipts",
    " \"$\{GUNBC_TREE_SRC\}/\" ", live_spec().service.repo_root as String, "/",
  ], "")
  let wrong_exec = join([
    "/usr/bin/rsync -rlpt --delete --exclude /target --exclude /.git --exclude /.claude",
    " --exclude /.gunbc --exclude /target-codex --exclude /.cargo-home",
    " --exclude /.gunbc-build-receipts --exclude /.gunbc-converge-receipts",
    " \"$\{GUNBC_TREE_SRC\}/\" ", wrong_root as String, "/",
  ], "")
  !has_directive(
    dirs: tree_sync_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: ExecStart { command: original_exec as NonEmptyStr })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: tree_sync_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: ExecStart { command: wrong_exec as NonEmptyStr })
}

// ── ADDITIONAL REDs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The spec_with_principal pattern: rebuild DeploymentServiceConfig field by field,
// changing only service_user to a divergent value.

fn spec_with_principal(wrong_user: NonEmptyStr) -> DeploymentSpec {
  let base = live_spec()
  match base {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
          instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr,
              service_user: wrong_user,
              service_uid: uid, listen: l,
              serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn serve_service_directives_of(spec: DeploymentSpec) -> List<SystemdServiceDirective> {
  match live_deploy_serve_unit_file(spec: spec, revision: live_revision()) {
    ServiceUnitFile { service: s, .. } => s
  }
}

// RED: a spec with a different principal makes belt_service_runs_as_service_principal go red.
// The emitter TRACKS the mutation: the wrong user IS present, the original user is ABSENT.
test fn belt_service_user_goes_red_under_different_principal() -> Bool {
  let wrong = join(["not-", live_spec().service.service_user as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_principal(wrong_user: wrong)
  !has_directive(
    dirs: belt_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: ServiceUser { name: live_spec().service.service_user })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: belt_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: ServiceUser { name: wrong })
}

// RED: same pattern for the serve unit.
test fn serve_unit_user_goes_red_under_different_principal() -> Bool {
  let wrong = join(["not-", live_spec().service.service_user as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_principal(wrong_user: wrong)
  !has_directive(
    dirs: serve_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: ServiceUser { name: live_spec().service.service_user })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: serve_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: ServiceUser { name: wrong })
}

// RED: same pattern for the tree-sync unit.
test fn tree_sync_user_goes_red_under_different_principal() -> Bool {
  let wrong = join(["not-", live_spec().service.service_user as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_principal(wrong_user: wrong)
  !has_directive(
    dirs: tree_sync_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: ServiceUser { name: live_spec().service.service_user })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: tree_sync_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: ServiceUser { name: wrong })
}

// RED: serve ExecStart goes red under a different port.
// The positive claim pins the port in the ExecStart string; changing it produces a mismatch.
fn spec_with_port(wrong_port: Port) -> DeploymentSpec {
  let base = live_spec()
  match base {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
          instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr, service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_function: f,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr,
              service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
              listen: HttpServerListenConfig { host: l.host, port: wrong_port },
              serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

test fn serve_unit_execstart_goes_red_under_different_port() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  let wrong_port = 8081 // a different port within the valid range
  let wrong_spec = spec_with_port(wrong_port: wrong_port)
  let original_exec = join([
    svc.serve_binary as String,
    " serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2",
    " --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag --function ", svc.serve_function as String,
    " --host ", svc.listen.host, " --port ", to_string(svc.listen.port),
    " --release-revision ", "b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd",
    " --eval-budget-cpu-ms 30000 --eval-budget-wall-ms 30000",
  ], "")
  let wrong_exec = join([
    svc.serve_binary as String,
    " serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2",
    " --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag --function ", svc.serve_function as String,
    " --host ", svc.listen.host, " --port ", to_string(wrong_port),
    " --release-revision ", "b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd",
    " --eval-budget-cpu-ms 30000 --eval-budget-wall-ms 30000",
  ], "")
  !has_directive(
    dirs: serve_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
    target: ExecStart { command: original_exec as NonEmptyStr })
    && has_directive(
      dirs: serve_service_directives_of(spec: wrong_spec),
      target: ExecStart { command: wrong_exec as NonEmptyStr })
}
